1
00:00:00,319 --> 00:00:03,159
Most people cannot lie for long without some

2
00:00:03,299 --> 00:00:06,159
cost. A tightness in the chest, a

3
00:00:06,219 --> 00:00:09,099
memory that resurfaces at three in the morning,

4
00:00:09,679 --> 00:00:13,119
a small private erosion that eventually pushes them

5
00:00:13,159 --> 00:00:16,099
toward confession, or at least towards stopping.

6
00:00:16,699 --> 00:00:19,100
Some people feel none of that. Not less

7
00:00:19,139 --> 00:00:22,199
of it, none of it. And understanding why

8
00:00:22,680 --> 00:00:24,559
changes how you should watch for danger in

9
00:00:24,600 --> 00:00:27,920
the first place. It also explains something that

10
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:31,500
confuses almost everyone the first time they encounter

11
00:00:31,539 --> 00:00:34,860
it up close. Why the most damaging liars

12
00:00:34,919 --> 00:00:38,180
are so rarely the nervous, shifty, obviously

13
00:00:38,279 --> 00:00:41,159
hiding something type we picture. They tend to

14
00:00:41,219 --> 00:00:43,639
be the calmest person in the room. This

15
00:00:43,719 --> 00:00:46,611
is Shadow Figure For most of us, guilt

16
00:00:46,731 --> 00:00:48,971
is not a punishment that arrives after the

17
00:00:49,071 --> 00:00:52,271
fact. It's a brake that engages before the

18
00:00:52,371 --> 00:00:55,231
act. A felt sense of, "I can't do

19
00:00:55,291 --> 00:00:58,191
this to another person," that shows up even

20
00:00:58,211 --> 00:01:00,811
when nobody is watching, even when there's no

21
00:01:00,951 --> 00:01:03,291
chance of being caught. It's one of the

22
00:01:03,351 --> 00:01:06,831
main reasons ordinary people don't lie, cheat,

23
00:01:07,431 --> 00:01:09,911
or exploit others as a matter of course.

24
00:01:10,511 --> 00:01:13,481
Not fear of consequences, but an internal

25
00:01:13,511 --> 00:01:16,511
signal that simply won't let them. In a

26
00:01:16,571 --> 00:01:20,071
smaller number of people, that brake is dramatically

27
00:01:20,111 --> 00:01:23,421
weaker or effectively absent. Clinical

28
00:01:23,431 --> 00:01:26,031
psychology describes this cluster of traits,

29
00:01:26,531 --> 00:01:29,891
superficial charm, a shallow or absent sense of

30
00:01:29,931 --> 00:01:33,071
remorse, a tendency to treat other people as

31
00:01:33,211 --> 00:01:35,891
instruments for getting what you want rather than

32
00:01:35,971 --> 00:01:38,791
as ends in themselves, under labels like

33
00:01:39,171 --> 00:01:42,431
antisocial personality traits or as part of what

34
00:01:42,471 --> 00:01:45,911
researchers call the dark triad, alongside

35
00:01:45,951 --> 00:01:48,771
narcissism and manipulation-focused traits,

36
00:01:49,351 --> 00:01:52,631
often grouped as Machiavellianism. It's worth being

37
00:01:52,651 --> 00:01:55,961
precise here. This exists on a spectrum, not

38
00:01:56,011 --> 00:01:58,251
as a single switch that's either on or

39
00:01:58,351 --> 00:02:00,691
off, and it is not the same thing

40
00:02:00,731 --> 00:02:03,131
as simply being a bad person in some

41
00:02:03,171 --> 00:02:05,731
vague moral sense. It's a specific,

42
00:02:06,151 --> 00:02:09,391
describable difference in how strongly that internal

43
00:02:09,491 --> 00:02:12,791
brake engages. It's also worth separating this

44
00:02:12,831 --> 00:02:16,151
from ordinary bad behavior borne of poor impulse

45
00:02:16,231 --> 00:02:19,011
control. Plenty of people do damaging,

46
00:02:19,371 --> 00:02:21,671
selfish things in the heat of the moment

47
00:02:21,731 --> 00:02:24,581
and then spend real time afterward wrestling with

48
00:02:24,631 --> 00:02:27,691
what they did. That struggle is itself

49
00:02:27,751 --> 00:02:30,781
evidence the brake exists and simply got

50
00:02:30,831 --> 00:02:33,951
overridden once. What we're describing here is

51
00:02:33,991 --> 00:02:37,431
different. Not an override under pressure, but

52
00:02:37,491 --> 00:02:40,071
a brake that was never wired in with

53
00:02:40,111 --> 00:02:43,591
the same strength to begin with, present or

54
00:02:43,651 --> 00:02:46,691
absent, regardless of how much time passes to

55
00:02:46,731 --> 00:02:49,771
reflect on it. Here's what makes it dangerous

56
00:02:50,071 --> 00:02:53,331
rather than just clinically interesting. The traits that

57
00:02:53,391 --> 00:02:56,391
go missing. Guilt, anxious self-doubt,

58
00:02:56,791 --> 00:03:00,251
fear of social disapproval are exactly the traits

59
00:03:00,291 --> 00:03:03,351
that usually make deception exhausting to sustain.

60
00:03:04,071 --> 00:03:06,551
Take them out and lying stops being a

61
00:03:06,591 --> 00:03:10,031
psychological cost at all. It becomes just another

62
00:03:10,091 --> 00:03:12,611
tool used as casually and as

63
00:03:12,631 --> 00:03:16,011
confidently as any other. That confidence

64
00:03:16,371 --> 00:03:18,411
is precisely what reads to the rest of

65
00:03:18,471 --> 00:03:21,909
us as charm, as certainty, as the calm

66
00:03:21,949 --> 00:03:24,669
of someone who clearly has nothing to hide

67
00:03:25,189 --> 00:03:27,519
when the calm is actually coming from the

68
00:03:27,529 --> 00:03:30,629
fact that nothing inside them is objecting to

69
00:03:30,669 --> 00:03:33,189
the hiding in the first place. There's a

70
00:03:33,249 --> 00:03:35,469
second layer to this that makes it even

71
00:03:35,529 --> 00:03:38,249
harder to detect in the moment. For most

72
00:03:38,289 --> 00:03:41,729
people, lying requires active cognitive effort.

73
00:03:42,329 --> 00:03:44,069
You have to track what you said before,

74
00:03:44,509 --> 00:03:47,169
suppress the truth, and manage your own visible

75
00:03:47,229 --> 00:03:50,289
discomfort all at once, which is part of

76
00:03:50,309 --> 00:03:53,669
why deception researchers can sometimes detect it through

77
00:03:53,729 --> 00:03:56,129
hesitation or inconsistency under

78
00:03:56,209 --> 00:03:59,709
pressure. Someone without that internal brake isn't

79
00:03:59,769 --> 00:04:02,609
fighting a suppressed truth or managing rising

80
00:04:02,649 --> 00:04:06,089
anxiety. There's simply less internal conflict to

81
00:04:06,129 --> 00:04:08,709
manage in the first place, which frees up

82
00:04:08,719 --> 00:04:11,249
mental bandwidth most liars don't have.

83
00:04:11,909 --> 00:04:15,149
Bandwidth that gets spent instead on reading you,

84
00:04:15,749 --> 00:04:18,529
adjusting the story in real time, and staying

85
00:04:18,589 --> 00:04:21,849
likable while doing it. The absence of guilt

86
00:04:21,889 --> 00:04:24,829
doesn't just remove a cost. It reallocates the

87
00:04:24,849 --> 00:04:28,229
resources that guilt would have consumed toward getting

88
00:04:28,269 --> 00:04:31,529
better at the manipulation itself. This is

89
00:04:31,609 --> 00:04:34,729
also why appeals to conscience so often

90
00:04:34,769 --> 00:04:37,699
fail with someone like this in a way

91
00:04:37,729 --> 00:04:40,549
that baffles the people trying them. Telling a

92
00:04:40,589 --> 00:04:43,799
genuinely remorseless person, "Think about what this is

93
00:04:43,829 --> 00:04:46,499
doing to the people who trusted you," assumes

94
00:04:46,529 --> 00:04:49,029
there's a lever there to pull. For most

95
00:04:49,069 --> 00:04:52,539
people, that sentence lands somewhere and produces

96
00:04:52,589 --> 00:04:55,939
real discomfort. For someone operating without

97
00:04:55,969 --> 00:04:59,249
that internal brake, the sentence is just information,

98
00:04:59,769 --> 00:05:02,289
data about how you expect them to feel,

99
00:05:02,729 --> 00:05:05,409
which they can now use, consciously or not,

100
00:05:05,889 --> 00:05:08,289
to perform the appropriate response back at you.

101
00:05:08,969 --> 00:05:11,109
It helps to separate two things that get

102
00:05:11,149 --> 00:05:14,609
confused constantly: fear of consequences

103
00:05:14,649 --> 00:05:17,749
and guilt itself. Plenty of people without a

104
00:05:17,829 --> 00:05:20,809
strong internal brake still avoid certain lies,

105
00:05:21,509 --> 00:05:24,119
not because lying feels wrong to them, but

106
00:05:24,149 --> 00:05:27,129
because getting caught carries a cost they'd rather

107
00:05:27,149 --> 00:05:30,589
not pay. That's risk management, not conscience,

108
00:05:31,189 --> 00:05:32,978
and the two can look identical from the

109
00:05:33,069 --> 00:05:36,329
outside right up until the risk calculation changes.

110
00:05:37,189 --> 00:05:39,949
Remove the audience, remove the paper trail,

111
00:05:40,349 --> 00:05:43,729
remove the chance of consequence, and risk management

112
00:05:43,809 --> 00:05:47,129
quietly disappears. Genuine guilt doesn't

113
00:05:47,189 --> 00:05:50,029
need an audience to function. That's the actual

114
00:05:50,149 --> 00:05:53,369
test hiding underneath all of this. Does the

115
00:05:53,409 --> 00:05:56,129
behavior hold up when nobody would ever find

116
00:05:56,189 --> 00:05:59,313
out either way? You've seen the outward signature

117
00:05:59,353 --> 00:06:01,683
of this before, even if you didn't have

118
00:06:01,733 --> 00:06:03,873
a name for it. Go back to the

119
00:06:03,933 --> 00:06:06,353
cases this channel has already covered.

120
00:06:07,173 --> 00:06:09,773
Bernie Madoff maintained a calm, steady,

121
00:06:10,213 --> 00:06:13,513
reassuring presence with investors for three decades,

122
00:06:13,993 --> 00:06:17,283
never once breaking character under direct scrutiny

123
00:06:17,283 --> 00:06:20,613
from regulators, journalists, or his own clients.

124
00:06:21,253 --> 00:06:23,823
A consistency that many people who dealt with

125
00:06:23,873 --> 00:06:26,723
him described after the fact as almost

126
00:06:26,773 --> 00:06:29,853
impossible to reconcile with what he'd actually been

127
00:06:29,893 --> 00:06:33,393
doing the entire time. Elizabeth Holmes, by

128
00:06:33,433 --> 00:06:36,853
multiple former colleagues' accounts, held unusually

129
00:06:36,873 --> 00:06:40,202
steady, unblinking eye contact through high-stakes

130
00:06:40,293 --> 00:06:43,793
meetings where the underlying technology was failing behind

131
00:06:43,833 --> 00:06:46,723
the scenes without any visible crack in her

132
00:06:46,723 --> 00:06:49,863
composure. To be clear, and this matters,

133
00:06:50,533 --> 00:06:53,783
neither of them has been clinically diagnosed with

134
00:06:53,853 --> 00:06:57,353
antisocial personality traits in any verified

135
00:06:57,653 --> 00:07:00,973
public way, and it would be irresponsible to

136
00:07:01,073 --> 00:07:03,833
state that as fact. What can be said

137
00:07:03,973 --> 00:07:06,613
honestly is narrower and more useful.

138
00:07:07,313 --> 00:07:10,273
Journalists, psychologists commenting publicly,

139
00:07:10,833 --> 00:07:13,923
and courtroom observers have repeatedly speculated

140
00:07:14,393 --> 00:07:17,673
based on documented behavior patterns like sustained

141
00:07:17,713 --> 00:07:21,113
composure under exposure, and a striking absence

142
00:07:21,153 --> 00:07:24,173
of visible remorse even after public collapse,

143
00:07:24,773 --> 00:07:27,293
that traits along this spectrum may have been

144
00:07:27,393 --> 00:07:30,613
present. That's public speculation about behavior,

145
00:07:31,133 --> 00:07:33,833
not a diagnosis of a person's mind, and

146
00:07:33,873 --> 00:07:36,693
the distinction matters because diagnosing

147
00:07:36,783 --> 00:07:40,053
someone you've never clinically evaluated is its

148
00:07:40,113 --> 00:07:43,373
own kind of overreach. What you can observe

149
00:07:43,413 --> 00:07:46,113
without diagnosing anyone is the pattern

150
00:07:46,153 --> 00:07:49,303
itself: a sustained lie that never

151
00:07:49,433 --> 00:07:52,773
seems to cost the liar anything visible, held

152
00:07:52,813 --> 00:07:56,053
together not by cleverness alone, but by the

153
00:07:56,133 --> 00:07:59,213
simple absence of the discomfort that would make

154
00:07:59,273 --> 00:08:02,633
most people flinch. Notice, too, how both

155
00:08:02,693 --> 00:08:06,013
cases collapsed the same way from the outside.

156
00:08:06,073 --> 00:08:08,693
Not through the person eventually breaking down and

157
00:08:08,733 --> 00:08:12,073
admitting it, but through outside evidence: bank

158
00:08:12,173 --> 00:08:15,053
statements, whistleblower testimony, a

159
00:08:15,153 --> 00:08:18,313
journalist's reporting forcing the truth into the

160
00:08:18,453 --> 00:08:21,883
open despite them. Madoff confessed only

161
00:08:21,973 --> 00:08:24,283
after the scheme had mathematically run out of

162
00:08:24,333 --> 00:08:27,293
new money to sustain itself, not because guilt

163
00:08:27,393 --> 00:08:29,813
finally caught up with him after thirty years.

164
00:08:30,293 --> 00:08:33,473
Holmes maintained her account of the technology's viability

165
00:08:33,993 --> 00:08:36,853
well after internal employees had already raised the

166
00:08:36,893 --> 00:08:40,373
alarm. In neither case did the internal signal

167
00:08:40,413 --> 00:08:43,693
we'd expect, a slow mounting need to come

168
00:08:43,733 --> 00:08:46,173
clean, appear to be the thing that ended

169
00:08:46,213 --> 00:08:49,693
it. This same pattern shows up far outside

170
00:08:49,752 --> 00:08:52,572
the world of nine-figure fraud, and that's really

171
00:08:52,632 --> 00:08:54,432
the point of putting it next to these

172
00:08:54,492 --> 00:08:57,812
two cases. The coworker who takes credit

173
00:08:57,852 --> 00:09:00,592
for someone else's work without a flicker of

174
00:09:00,652 --> 00:09:04,092
hesitation. The partner who has an entire

175
00:09:04,252 --> 00:09:07,613
second life running in parallel without any of

176
00:09:07,652 --> 00:09:10,853
the strain we assume secrecy should produce. The

177
00:09:10,912 --> 00:09:14,212
friend who borrows money with complete sincerity and

178
00:09:14,273 --> 00:09:17,092
simply never mentions it again. None of these

179
00:09:17,172 --> 00:09:20,492
situations require a criminal mastermind. They

180
00:09:20,592 --> 00:09:23,652
only require the same narrow trait, an

181
00:09:23,712 --> 00:09:27,133
internal brake that isn't doing what everyone around

182
00:09:27,172 --> 00:09:29,738
them assumes it must be doing. Here's the

183
00:09:29,808 --> 00:09:32,768
practical problem this creates for you. Most of

184
00:09:32,828 --> 00:09:35,488
us are quietly trained to wait for guilt

185
00:09:35,568 --> 00:09:38,368
as a warning sign. We expect a liar

186
00:09:38,408 --> 00:09:41,488
to eventually look away, to stumble, to show

187
00:09:41,548 --> 00:09:43,688
some flicker of conscience we can point to

188
00:09:44,148 --> 00:09:47,428
and say, "There, that's the tell." We

189
00:09:47,468 --> 00:09:49,568
wait for the confession that proves we were

190
00:09:49,648 --> 00:09:52,798
right to be suspicious. For a person who

191
00:09:52,928 --> 00:09:55,828
genuinely doesn't feel that internal break, that

192
00:09:55,888 --> 00:09:59,358
flicker may never come. Waiting for remorse

193
00:09:59,768 --> 00:10:02,328
as your early warning system means you may

194
00:10:02,368 --> 00:10:04,988
simply never get a warning at all, right

195
00:10:05,068 --> 00:10:07,548
up until the damage is already done and

196
00:10:07,608 --> 00:10:10,797
irreversible. This is exactly why the earlier

197
00:10:10,888 --> 00:10:13,488
episodes in this series keep returning to the

198
00:10:13,528 --> 00:10:16,808
same underlying advice. Look at documented

199
00:10:16,848 --> 00:10:20,048
behavior over time, not promises about future

200
00:10:20,088 --> 00:10:22,948
behavior, and not the emotional performance in the

201
00:10:23,028 --> 00:10:26,028
room with you. Two patterns are worth watching

202
00:10:26,108 --> 00:10:29,408
specifically. First, how someone treats

203
00:10:29,488 --> 00:10:31,688
people who can no longer do anything for

204
00:10:31,788 --> 00:10:35,128
them: a former partner, an employee who's

205
00:10:35,168 --> 00:10:37,738
been let go, a friend who's no longer

206
00:10:37,788 --> 00:10:40,998
useful. Genuine remorse tends to show up

207
00:10:41,108 --> 00:10:44,508
as ongoing discomfort about past harm even

208
00:10:44,528 --> 00:10:47,668
years later. Its absence tends to show up

209
00:10:47,788 --> 00:10:50,688
as a clean, total indifference the moment

210
00:10:50,768 --> 00:10:54,048
usefulness ends. Second, watch what

211
00:10:54,108 --> 00:10:56,388
happens when someone is caught in a small,

212
00:10:56,708 --> 00:10:59,968
low-stakes lie. Not the big one, a minor

213
00:11:00,028 --> 00:11:02,708
one. Do they show even a flicker of

214
00:11:02,768 --> 00:11:06,248
visible discomfort? Or do they pivot instantly

215
00:11:06,288 --> 00:11:09,708
and smoothly to the next explanation without so

216
00:11:09,788 --> 00:11:12,708
much as a beat of hesitation? That second

217
00:11:12,748 --> 00:11:16,008
reaction, repeated consistently across small moments,

218
00:11:16,378 --> 00:11:18,848
tells you more than any grand confession ever

219
00:11:18,948 --> 00:11:22,408
will. A third pattern worth adding, listen for

220
00:11:22,468 --> 00:11:25,958
how they narrate their own past conflicts. Genuine

221
00:11:26,028 --> 00:11:29,328
remorse tends to produce complicated, slightly

222
00:11:29,488 --> 00:11:32,448
uncomfortable self-accounts. "I handled that

223
00:11:32,488 --> 00:11:34,618
badly. I was wrong about that for a

224
00:11:34,688 --> 00:11:38,008
long time." A narrative that is suspiciously

225
00:11:38,068 --> 00:11:41,068
clean, where they are consistently the reasonable

226
00:11:41,168 --> 00:11:44,388
one and every past conflict was caused

227
00:11:44,448 --> 00:11:47,588
entirely by someone else's instability or

228
00:11:47,668 --> 00:11:51,048
unfairness is worth noticing. It doesn't

229
00:11:51,088 --> 00:11:53,368
prove anything on its own the first time

230
00:11:53,408 --> 00:11:56,408
you hear it. As a repeated pattern across

231
00:11:56,498 --> 00:11:59,728
many different relationships and situations, it

232
00:11:59,808 --> 00:12:02,757
starts to describe something real about how that

233
00:12:02,788 --> 00:12:05,689
person actually processes their own conduct.

234
00:12:06,269 --> 00:12:08,979
None of this means treating everyone with unusual

235
00:12:09,089 --> 00:12:12,469
composure as suspect. Plenty of confident,

236
00:12:12,749 --> 00:12:15,369
calm people are exactly what they appear to

237
00:12:15,409 --> 00:12:18,769
be. It means not outsourcing your judgment to

238
00:12:18,829 --> 00:12:21,629
the expectation that guilt will eventually surface on

239
00:12:21,689 --> 00:12:24,759
its own. For some people, it simply won't,

240
00:12:25,309 --> 00:12:27,849
and building your safety around waiting for it

241
00:12:27,909 --> 00:12:30,009
is building on a signal that for the

242
00:12:30,069 --> 00:12:32,289
person in front of you may not exist

243
00:12:32,349 --> 00:12:35,249
at all. There's a related trap worth naming

244
00:12:35,289 --> 00:12:38,729
directly, mistaking a good apology for genuine

245
00:12:38,749 --> 00:12:42,059
remorse. Someone without a strong internal

246
00:12:42,169 --> 00:12:45,149
brake can still learn through pure observation

247
00:12:45,549 --> 00:12:49,049
exactly what a sincere-sounding apology looks and sounds

248
00:12:49,109 --> 00:12:52,069
like. The right words, the right pause,

249
00:12:52,549 --> 00:12:55,969
even something that resembles tears. The test isn't

250
00:12:56,009 --> 00:12:58,349
whether the apology sounds right in the moment

251
00:12:58,389 --> 00:13:01,469
it's delivered. The test is whether the underlying

252
00:13:01,549 --> 00:13:04,089
behavior actually changes afterward

253
00:13:04,099 --> 00:13:06,649
consistently without you having to keep

254
00:13:06,689 --> 00:13:09,509
checking. Words cost nothing to produce.

255
00:13:10,269 --> 00:13:13,109
A sustained change in behavior over weeks and

256
00:13:13,149 --> 00:13:16,259
months is the only version of sorry that

257
00:13:16,309 --> 00:13:19,699
carries real information. This also reframes

258
00:13:19,729 --> 00:13:22,709
what trusting your gut should actually mean

259
00:13:22,929 --> 00:13:25,889
around someone like this. Your gut is very

260
00:13:25,929 --> 00:13:28,879
good at picking up on charm, confidence, and

261
00:13:28,989 --> 00:13:32,329
